      "content": "Please note: German tenancy law (Wohngesetz / B_ARGV) generally places the responsibility for maintaining the building's heating system on the landlord, and attempting to make a tenant prove they didn't cause the damage could be legally unwise or counterproductive in your favor. Including self-accusation or excessive doubt about the source might unintentionally weaken your claim. It is fully clear that the issue stems from normal wear of the equipment, but suggesting \"something internal fails\" implies intent by the landlord rather than stating it objectively as a maintenance obligation.\n\nHere is the revised, stronger version that states the facts clearly and confidently without implying suspicion of internal damage by the tenant.\n\n**Subject:** URGENT: Mechanical Failure in Living Room Radiator – Tenancy address [Your Address]\n\nDear Tenant Administration Team,\n\nI am formally reporting a critical mechanical failure in the living room radiator, characterized by a distinct rattling click that occurs immediately before the unit stops heating effectively, which has left the apartment dangerously cold. This specific auditory anomaly clearly indicates a mechanical fault requiring an immediate on-site diagnosis, as the device has been operated without any deviation from normal use conditions.\n\n**Please note that the unit has served without interruption under",
